  "page1": {
    "title": "When Things Disappear in Water",
    "subtitle": "Sugar and salt seem to vanish, but they are still right there.",
    "body": "Drop some sugar into a glass of cool water. Stir it slowly with a spoon. Soon the small white sugar seems to disappear. But it is not gone at all.\n\nThe sugar breaks into very tiny bits. These bits spread all through the clear water. We say the sugar <u>dissolves</u> in the water. The clear mix is now a sweet solution.\n\nSalt and many powders can dissolve too. Some things dissolve, and some do not. Warm water can dissolve more sugar than cool water. The heat helps the tiny bits move fast.\n\nStirring also helps the sugar dissolve faster. So warm water and stirring <mark>speed things up</mark>. Taste a drop, and you will know. The sweet sugar is still right there.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/mars/2026-07/11.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "Sugar swirling into a glass of water and spreading into a clear, sweet solution."
  },
